Ibn Sa'di's commentary on Surah 17, verses 41–44:

{41} He, exalted is He, informs that He has varied (sarrafa) in this Qur'an for His servants; that is, He diversified the rulings, clarified them, and multiplied the proofs and evidences for what He called to, and He admonished and reminded so that they would take heed of what benefits them, so they would follow it, and of what harms them, so they would avoid it. But most people refused, except in aversion (nufur) from the signs of God, out of their hatred for the truth and their love for what they were upon of falsehood, until they became fanatically attached to their falsehood, and did not lend the signs of God an ear, nor did they give them any attention.

{42} Among the greatest matters in which He varied the signs and evidences is Tawhid (the affirmation of God's oneness), which is the foundation of foundations. So He commanded it, forbade its opposite, and established upon it a great many rational and transmitted proofs, such that whoever lends an ear to some of them will not leave any doubt or uncertainty in his heart. Among the proofs for this is the rational proof mentioned here, so He said: {Say}: to the polytheists who ascribe with God another god: {If there were gods with Him, as they say}; that is, according to the requirement of their claim and fabrication; {then they would have sought a way to the Lord of the Throne}; that is, they would have sought a way to God through worship of Him, turning to Him, drawing near to Him, and seeking the means (wasila). So how can the servant, poor and needy, who sees the intensity of his need for the servitude of his Lord, be made a god alongside God? Is this not among the greatest of injustices and the most profound of foolishness? On this meaning, this verse is like His saying, exalted is He: {Those whom they call upon seek the means of access to their Lord, which of them is nearest} and like His saying, exalted is He: {And on the Day He will gather them and what they worshipped besides God, and He will say, "Did you lead these servants of Mine astray, or did they themselves stray from the way?" They will say, "Exalted are You! It was not befitting us to take any protectors besides You."}

It is also possible that the meaning of His saying: {Say: If there were gods with Him, as they say, then they would have sought a way to the Lord of the Throne} is: they would have sought the way and striven to overpower God, exalted is He. Then either they would prevail over Him, so that whoever prevails and overpowers would be the Lord and God; but since they know that they acknowledge that the gods they call upon besides God are subdued and overpowered, having no share of the affair, why then did they take them while they are in this state? This is like His saying, exalted is He: {God has not taken any son, and there is not with Him any god; otherwise each god would have taken away what he created, and some of them would have overcome others.}

{43} {Glorified is He and exalted}; that is, sanctified, far removed from imperfection, and His attributes are high, {above what they say}: of associating others with Him and taking rivals alongside Him, {an exalted highness}: so His station is elevated, His greatness is immense, and His majesty, which cannot be conceived of as having gods with Him, is exalted. Whoever says that has indeed strayed into manifest error and committed a great injustice. The great created things have shrunk before His greatness, and the seven heavens and whoever is in them and the seven earths and whoever is in them have become small before His majesty; the earth entire is His grip on the Day of Resurrection, and the heavens are folded up in His right hand. The higher and lower worlds are in need of Him with an essential need that does not separate from any of them at any moment. This need is in all its aspects: a need in terms of creation, provision, and management, and a need in terms of being compelled to make Him the one worshipped and beloved, toward whom they draw near, and to whom they turn in every state for refuge.

{44} For this reason He said: {The seven heavens and the earth and whoever is in them glorify Him, and there is not a thing}: of living rational and non-rational beings, of trees, plants, and inanimate things, living and dead, {except that it glorifies Him with His praise}: by the tongue of disposition and by the tongue of speech, {but you do not understand their glorification}; that is, the glorification of the remaining created things, which are of a language other than yours. Rather, the All-Knower of the unseen encompasses them. {Indeed, He is ever Forbearing, Forgiving}: for He does not hasten punishment against one who said about Him a saying by which the heavens might almost be torn apart, the earth split open, and the mountains collapse. Rather, He gave them respite, bestowed favor upon them, granted them well-being, provided for them, and called them to His door so that they might repent of this great sin, that He might give them abundant reward and forgive them their sin. Had it not been for His forbearance and forgiveness, the heavens would have fallen upon the earth, and He would not have left upon its back a single creature.

